<section class="panel" aria-label="Source-grounded Number Line Scoot and non-unit fractions lesson7">
<h2>Count One-fourths Through Four Wholes</h2><p>Count one fourth per step. The source warm-up has four rows of four terms, not one row that stops at 1.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Original row</th><th scope="col">Four successive fractions</th><th scope="col">Whole at row end</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 4/4</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>5/4, 6/4, 7/4, 8/4</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>9/4, 10/4, 11/4, 12/4</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>13/4, 14/4, 15/4, 16/4</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p><strong>Denominator</strong> 4 means each whole has four equal parts. <strong>Numerator</strong> is the count of those parts. This source lesson introduces those names.</p>
<h2>Activity 1 — Number Line Scoot, Stage 2</h2>
<p>Two players, number cube, gamepieces and source center gameboard. Each piece starts at zero on a chosen fraction line. Record the roll and move that many unit-fraction spaces. Reach <strong>exactly 4</strong> with as many markers as you can. The publisher Stage 2 blackline gameboard and directions must be checked by the teacher, and are not reproduced.</p>
<p>Illustrative independent check: from 3/4, move four fourths, landing at 7/4, <strong>not</strong> at 4. The game winner depends on real student play.</p>
<h2>Activity 2 — Eight source fraction placements in four pairs</h2><p>The four original problems place each pair on the <em>same</em> 0-to-2 line. Each shaded interval below is one unit fraction, and the endpoint is the fraction number.</p>
